<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Summary of previous work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The filled and open circles in the bottom row of Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> represent the results mentioned in the first paragraph of the paper.
A full account of the known cases where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> by Keel in 1992 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math> by Belorousski in 1998 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> (Chow); Petersen in 2014 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> (cohomology),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Mumford in 1983 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> by Faber in 1988 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Faber in 1990 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> by excision. The weaker statement that <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is pictured by open circles <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> and was previously known for:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Faber in 1990 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Izadi in 1995 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Penev and Vakil in 2015 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=7,8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=7,8,9</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I2.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by the authors in 2021 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Integral coefficients)<span id="S1.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Determining the Chow ring of <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with integral coefficients is a much more subtle problem. The cases where the integral Chow ring is known are <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by Keel in 1992 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>; <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> by Edidin–Graham in 1998 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>; <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by E. Larson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> in 2021; <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,1}</annotation></semantics></math> by Di Lorenzo, Pernice, and Vistoli in 2021 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are also cases where results are known in cohomology but not in Chow.
Much of the previous work computing <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> has used point counting.
The number of points <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the sum over isomorphism classes of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> points, weighted by size of the automorphism group.
We say <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has <em id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polynomial point count</em> if there exists a polynomial <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> with integer coefficients such that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=P(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=P(q)</annotation></semantics></math> for all prime powers <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has polynomial point count (see Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm15" title="Proposition 3.15. ‣ 3.2.1. Tautological cohomology and polynomial point counts ‣ 3.2. The cycle class map ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.15</span></a>).
On the other hand, if <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has polynomial point count, then all odd cohomology vanishes, and knowing the polynomial
determines the dimensions of all cohomology groups. Previously, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> was shown to have polynomial point count (and the polynomial determined) for</p>
<ul id="S1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I3.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\top" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.500em">⊤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\top</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 7</annotation></semantics></math> by Bergström in 2009 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I3.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\top" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.500em">⊤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\top</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> by Getzler–Looijenga in 1999 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>; <math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> by Bergström in 2008 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I3.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\top" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.500em">⊤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\top</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I3.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by
Bergström and Tommasi in 2006 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>; <math id="S1.I3.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math> by Bergström, Faber and Payne in 2022
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Upon completing this manuscript, the authors learned that Begström and Faber have improved the first two cases above to <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 7</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Having polynomial point count does not rule out the possibility of non-tautological classes in even degrees, but with some additional work, it is known that all cohomology is tautological in the following cases:</p>
<ul id="S1.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I4.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacktriangledown" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.500em" mathvariant="normal">▼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacktriangledown</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I4.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I4.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I4.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> by work of Petersen and Tommasi in 2016 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, which proved all even cohomology is tautological for <math id="S1.I4.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I4.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;20</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacktriangledown" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.500em" mathvariant="normal">▼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacktriangledown</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I4.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I4.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I4.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I4.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I4.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Schmitt and van Zelm in 2020 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In the remaining genus <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cases where polynomial point count is known, it is expected that all cohomology is tautological.
While computer calculations in low codimension support this expectation, confirming it by computer seems beyond present techniques (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Remark 1.8]</cite>).
One consequence of our theorem will be confirmation that these polynomial point counts, as well as many new ones, arise because all cohomology is tautological.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, non-tautological classes have been discovered in the following cases:</p>
<ul id="S1.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I5.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I5.ix1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> <math id="S1.I5.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I5.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>. The weight <math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> discriminant cusp form gives rise to a holomorphic differential form on <math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Section 2.3]</cite> for an exposition).
Hence, <math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, giving non-tautological cohomology.
Moreover, by a theorem of Roĭtman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>,
existence of a holomorphic form implies that the group of zero-cycles in Chow is infinite-dimensional, so they cannot all be tautological. (See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> for an discussion of which points on <math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> can give tautological zero cycles.) Because pullback along the forgetful maps <math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective on Chow and cohomology, the existence of non-tautological classes propagates upward to all <math id="S1.I5.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I5.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I5.ix2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> <math id="S1.I5.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I5.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I5.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I5.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I5.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 14</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I5.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I5.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 15</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, there are known holomorphic differential forms on <math id="S1.I5.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,14}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,14}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Section 3.5]</cite>) and <math id="S1.I5.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{3,15}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I5.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I5.ix3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> <math id="S1.I5.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I5.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any <math id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 24</annotation></semantics></math>.
Graber–Pandharipande <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> produced the first explicit non-tautological <em id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">algebraic</em> cycle (in Chow and cohomology) on <math id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,20}</annotation></semantics></math>, the fundamental class of the bielliptic locus with <math id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> pairs of conjugate markings.
Generalizing these techniques, van Zelm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> then produced non-tautological algebraic cycles on <math id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.I5.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 24</annotation></semantics></math>, arising again from the bielliptic loci.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I5.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I5.ix4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> <math id="S1.I5.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diagdown" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" mathvariant="normal">╲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diagdown</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I5.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I5.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I5.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2,3</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.I5.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>. Upon completing this manuscript, the authors learned that <math id="S1.I5.ix4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> in these cases by work of Bergström and Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I5.ix5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I5.ix5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> <math id="S1.I5.ix5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diagdown" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" mathvariant="normal">╲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diagdown</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I5.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I5.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also note an interesting result of Pikaart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> which says <math id="S1.I5.ix5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.I5.ix5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Statement of main result</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result contributes 32 new cases, pictured by filled blue dots <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"> <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Upon restricting to the locus of compact type curves <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a few additional cases in Chow where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct})</annotation></semantics></math> (pictured by bold open circles <span id="S1.SS2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="100.00" height="100.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,100.00) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(8.3,4.15)"><circle fill="#000000" r="3.46" stroke="none" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g><g transform="translate(8.3,4.15)"><circle fill="#000000" r="1.38" stroke="none" stroke-width="0.4" x="0" y="0"/></g></g></svg></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Chow/cohomology rings of the following moduli spaces are generated by tautological classes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"></th>
<th id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"></span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"></span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"></span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"></span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"></span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"></span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=7</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.Thmthm4.p2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 6.0pt;"><math id="S1.Thmthm4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math></td>
