</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, for the <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> in the top row, the cycle class map <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Statement of main result ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> has immediate implications for point counting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The moduli space <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has polynomial point count for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Considering contributions from the boundary as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Proposition 4.2]</cite>, we also obtain point counting results for the following open moduli spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The moduli space <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has polynomial point count for all <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Applications for all <math id="S1.Thmthm7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S1.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cases in the top row of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Statement of main result ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> will serve as crucial base cases for an inductive argument describing
the cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in low degrees for all <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math>.
It was previously shown that <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,3,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,3,5</annotation></semantics></math> by Arbarello and Cornalba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. This was recently extended to <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=7,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=7,9</annotation></semantics></math> by Bergström, Faber and Payne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, where the key new input was knowing <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{4,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{4,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has polynomial point count for <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Forthcoming work of the authors and Sam Payne will show that
<math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is of Hodge–Tate type for all <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> and even <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Overview of the new techniques</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Statement of main result ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> with a novel method, which allows us to take advantage of the flexibility of excision in Chow, yet still obtain results in cohomology at the end. The key idea is prove our spaces have the <em id="S1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chow–Künneth generation Property (CKgP)</em>, which is a way of making up for the lack of a Künneth formula in Chow. We say <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP if for all <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a surjection</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)\otimes A^{*}(Y)\rightarrow A^{*}(X\times Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)\otimes A^{*}(Y)\rightarrow A^{*}(X\times Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Below are some important observations about the CKgP, which pave the way for our approach. Each italicized statement is not difficult to establish (and some have already appeared in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>), but they combine into a very powerful tool when applied to moduli spaces of curves:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The CKgP plays well with stratifications, products, and finite group quotients.</span> Thus, using the inductive nature of its boundary, we can reduce showing <math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> to showing the open moduli spaces <math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> have the CKgP and <math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\leq g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n</annotation></semantics></math> (as explained in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Filling criteria ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and surjective, and <math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, then <math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP</span>. Thus, to attack each piece <math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can pass from studying its gonality strata to studying Hurwitz spaces of covers with marked points (see Sections <a href="#S8" title="8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>–<a href="#S10" title="10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> is open and <math id="S1.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, then <math id="S1.I6.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP</span>. Often, we can construct a moduli space of marked curves (or curves with a map to <math id="S1.I6.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.I6.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) as a quotient of affine space minus some discriminant locus. In contrast with having polynomial point count or knowing generators in cohomology, we may throw out <em id="S1.I6.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arbitrary</em> closed subsets and still preserve CKgP and generators in Chow.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I6.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.I6.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper and has the CKgP, then the cycle class map is an isomorphism.</span> In other words — no matter what techniques from Chow we have used in (<a href="#S1.I6.i2" title="item 2 ‣ 1.3. Overview of the new techniques ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.I6.i3" title="item 3 ‣ 1.3. Overview of the new techniques ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) — so long as we put our pieces back together into a smooth, proper space, we now also have access to cohomology.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Part (1) above naturally leads us prove results for the collection of <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq a</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> (a triangular region bounded by the axes and a line of slope <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math>).
From this perspective, one should hope to prove
<math id="S1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> (the largest such triangular region that does not contain <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Statement of main result ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> confirms this hope, but also provides some answers to Questions <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Question 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Question 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> <em id="S1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">above</em> this region. Obtaining filled circles with <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n&gt;12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n&gt;12</annotation></semantics></math>, such as <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>, requires some more delicate, ad hoc arguments, and we view the results in this region as more surprising.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>The Gorenstein property</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having determined many cases where tautological classes generate the Chow ring, a natural next question is what are the relations among them?
Pixton conjectured a set of relations among the generators of the tautological ring, which were later proven to hold by Pandharipande–Pixton–Zvonkine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> (in cohomology) and Janda <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> (in the Chow ring). Pixton’s relations restrict to the Faber–Zagier relations on <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and one can also restrict Pixton’s relations on <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\ct}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\ct}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\rt}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>rt</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\rt}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. We will let <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ring generated by tautological classes modulo Pixton’s relations, where <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is any of the above moduli spaces of curves. Pixton has conjectured that <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{P}^{*}(\mathcal{M})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{P}^{*}(\mathcal{M})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is known that <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}(\mathcal{M})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}(\mathcal{M})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{d}(\mathcal{M})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{d}(\mathcal{M})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> where</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:=\begin{cases}g-2+n-\delta_{0,g}&amp;\text{if $\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\rt}$}\\
2g-3+n&amp;\text{if $\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct}$}\\
3g-3+n&amp;\text{if $\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}$.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>rt</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:=\begin{cases}g-2+n-\delta_{0,g}&amp;\text{if $\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\rt}$}\\
2g-3+n&amp;\text{if $\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct}$}\\
3g-3+n&amp;\text{if $\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}$.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">One of the prominent questions asked about the tautological ring is if <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein with socle in codimension <math id="S1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning there is a perfect pairing</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}(\mathcal{M})\times R^{d-i}(\mathcal{M})\to R^{d}(\mathcal{M})\cong\mathbb{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}(\mathcal{M})\times R^{d-i}(\mathcal{M})\to R^{d}(\mathcal{M})\cong\mathbb{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Gorenstein property is known to hold for <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}_{P}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}_{P}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to
black filled circles on the left of Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, as well as for <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 23</annotation></semantics></math> by Faber. In these cases, it was computationally proven that <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein using the Sage package admcycles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>; this in turn implies <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M})=R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M})=R^{*}_{P}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, since the perfect pairing prohibits any further relations. Although it was previously speculated that <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> may be Gorenstein <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, it is known that <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{P}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{P}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is not Gorenstein for <math id="S1.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 24</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the Gorenstein property was shown to fail by Petersen and Tommasi for <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 20</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as
<math id="S1.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2,n}^{\ct})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.
Pixton has also computationally shown <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{P}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\rt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>rt</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{P}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\rt})</annotation></semantics></math> is not Gorenstein in some other cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, Appendix A]</cite>. However, a case of Gorenstein failure has yet to be proven for <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\rt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>rt</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\rt})</annotation></semantics></math> (which includes the case of <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>). See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> for further discussion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Using Poincaré duality, the <math id="S1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> in the top row of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Statement of main result ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> provide many new cases where
the Gorenstein property does hold in Chow and cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In these cases, the ring is (in theory) completely determined:
a class is zero if and only if it pairs to zero with all generators in complementary dimension. The pairing on generators of <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is can be effectively computed (as explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Section 1.5]</cite>) and has been implemented in admcycles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 1.8. ‣ 1.4. The Gorenstein property ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> also provides a large region
on which to test Pixton’s conjectures: if <math id="S1.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}_{P}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}_{P}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is not Gorenstein in one of these cases, then a relation is missing.
We therefore propose the cases in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Statement of main result ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> as being of particular interest for future computational study.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.5. </span>Outline of the paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Preliminaries on the tautological ring ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we discuss the basics of the tautological rings. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we discuss the Chow–Künneth generation property, which is a key property for studying the boundary of <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Filling criteria ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we explain the inductive method. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Genus 
        
          0
        
       and 
        
          1 ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we summarize previous results on the moduli of pointed curves of genus <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. In Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Genus 2 and hyperelliptic curves in general ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we recall results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> to deal with the hyperelliptic locus (which completes everything in genus <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>). In Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Plane curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we give a quotient stack presentation for certain stacks of pointed plane curves (which can be used to complete the cases in genus <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>). In Section <a href="#S8" title="8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we introduce the Faber–Pandharipande–Hurwitz cycles, which are tautological cycles associated to certain finite covers of <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In Sections <a href="#S9" title="9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#S10" title="10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we show that the Chow rings of certain loci of pointed trigonal and tetragonal curves are generated by these cycles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.6. </span>Notations and conventions.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All schemes are taken over a fixed algebraically closed field of characteristic <math id="S1.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or greater than <math id="S1.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. Stacks are fibered in groupoids over the category of schemes over the base field (or ring). All Chow rings are taken with rational coefficients. We use the subspace convention for projective bundles and Grassmann bundles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgments</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are grateful to Elham Izadi, Kiran Kedlaya, Jun Lau, Dan Petersen, David Stapleton, and Ravi Vakil for helpful conversations. We thank Burt Totaro for pointing us to the references <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, which inspired Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Lemma 3.11. ‣ 3.2. The cycle class map ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>.
We thank Sam Payne for explaining the connection between cohomology and point counting. We are grateful to Rahul Pandharipande, Dan Petersen, and Johannes Schmitt for comments on an earlier version of the manuscript. We also thank Johannes Schmitt for his explanations about and work on admcycles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
We thank Jonas Bergström and Carel Faber for their comments and for sharing an early version of their work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> with us. We are especially grateful to Carel Faber for his extensive reading and comments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries on the tautological ring</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>The stable graph stratification</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The moduli space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> of stable curves of genus <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points is stratified by topological type. Associated to each topological type is a stable graph <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. To a stable graph <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, one associates the moduli space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of points in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with dual graph <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The disjoint union</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}=\coprod\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∐</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}=\coprod\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is called the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable graph stratification</em>.
We will also consider the space</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\prod_{v}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g(v),n(v)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>v</mi></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}=\prod_{v}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g(v),n(v)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of the stable graph <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an associated morphism</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">whose image is the closure of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. The spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> are in fact the union of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for graphs <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
that can be taken to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> via a sequence of edge contractions.
A union of strata <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\Gamma\in S}\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\Gamma\in S}\mathcal{M}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is open if and only if the set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is closed under contractions. (Contracting as far as possible, one gets to the graph with a single vertex of genus <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Tautological rings</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Roughly speaking, the tautological ring <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-subalgebra of the Chow ring generated by the “naturally occurring” cycles on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are several natural morphisms between the moduli spaces of stable curves with marked points <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vary. First, there are the forgetful morphisms</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{j}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{j}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">obtained by forgetting the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\mathrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\mathrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> marking. In addition, there are the gluing morphisms</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">as in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1. The stable graph stratification ‣ 2. Preliminaries on the tautological ring ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) for stable graphs <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system of tautological rings <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of smallest <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-subalgebras of the Chow rings <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> containing the unit elements <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}]</annotation></semantics></math> and that is closed under the pushforwards by the forgetful morphisms <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the gluing morphisms <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> for each stable graph <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be an open substack of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The tautological ring <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the image of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> under the natural restriction map <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow A^{*}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow A^{*}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The tautological ring in cohomology <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the image of <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> under the cycle class map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\rightarrow H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The tautological rings are also closed under pullbacks along the forgetful and gluing maps (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Section 0.3]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is also useful to have a more explicit description of the tautological ring for a given pair <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve, which comes equipped with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sections <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\mathrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\mathrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> marked point. We define the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> classes as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}=\sigma_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{f})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}=\sigma_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{f}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Under the identification of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the universal curve map <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\overline{\mathcal{C}}_{g,n}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the map forgetting the last marked point. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, we set</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{j}=f_{*}(\psi_{n+1}^{j+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{j}=f_{*}(\psi_{n+1}^{j+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> classes are the Chern classes of the Hodge bundle</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}:=c_{i}(f_{*}\omega_{f})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}:=c_{i}(f_{*}\omega_{f}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">They are expressed in terms of the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> classes with rational coefficients using Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> be a polynomial in <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> classes on <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pushforward of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a decorated stratum class on <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The decorated stratum classes provide generators for <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, Proposition 11]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The Chow–Künneth generation property</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because of the nature of the stable graph stratification, we will want to compute generators for Chow rings of spaces <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\prod_{i}\mathcal{M}_{g_{i},n_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∏</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\prod_{i}\mathcal{M}_{g_{i},n_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>. But unlike for cohomology, there is no Künneth formula in general for Chow rings, so it is not obvious how the exterior product maps</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigotimes_{i}A_{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g_{i},n_{i}})\rightarrow A_{*}(\prod_{i}\mathcal{M}_{g_{i},n_{i}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigotimes_{i}A_{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g_{i},n_{i}})\rightarrow A_{*}(\prod_{i}\mathcal{M}_{g_{i},n_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">behave.
In this section, we study when the exterior product map</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)\rightarrow A_{*}(Y\times X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)\rightarrow A_{*}(Y\times X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is surjective.
All of the stacks we need to consider later in the paper are finite type over the base field and admit a stratification by global quotient stacks. So throughout this section, we assume that all stacks are of this form. All Deligne–Mumford stacks admit a stratification by global quotient stacks (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Proposition 4.5.5]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following definition is taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Definition 2.5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has the <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chow–Künneth generation Property</em> (CKgP, for short) if for all algebraic stacks <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (of finite type and admitting a stratification by global quotient stacks), the exterior product map</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)\to A_{*}(Y\times X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)\to A_{*}(Y\times X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is surjective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Basic lemmas</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collect below several basic lemmas about the CKgP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have the CKgP. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}\times Y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}\times Y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP. More generally, if <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> have CKgP then <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{1}\times\cdots\times Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}\times\cdots\times Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has CKgP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first claim is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Lemma 2.8]</cite>, and the second claim follows by induction on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math> is open. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we have a commuting square</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="72.84" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-36.42px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 248.03 72.84" width="248.03"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,72.84) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(124.02,0) translate(0,36.42)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -124.01 -27.9)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 72.83)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(53.74,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -47.79 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="95.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 107.49 0) translate(86.88,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -47.71 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="95.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(U)\otimes A_{*}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(U)\otimes A_{*}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 64.31)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 13.75 0) translate(39.99,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.04 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="68.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(Y\times X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(Y\times X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 119.32 0) translate(75.05,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.88 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.19em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="71.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p3.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(U\times X).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(U\times X).}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -70.27 12.18 L -70.27 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -70.27 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -16.25 24.44 L 15.85 24.44"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.13 24.44)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 70.35 12.18 L 70.35 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 70.35 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -30 -24.44 L 27.68 -24.44"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 27.96 -24.44)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">The composition</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)\rightarrow A_{*}(Y\times X)\rightarrow A_{*}(U\times X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)\rightarrow A_{*}(Y\times X)\rightarrow A_{*}(U\times X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">is surjective by the fact that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and excision. It follows that the right vertical arrow
is also surjective.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> admits a finite stratification <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\coprod_{S\in\mathcal{S}}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\coprod_{S\in\mathcal{S}}S</annotation></semantics></math> such that each <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We induct on the size of the stratification, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. The case <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\mathcal{S}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\mathcal{S}=1</annotation></semantics></math> is clear. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a minimal element of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which exists because <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. Set <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=Y\smallsetminus T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=Y\smallsetminus T</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be any finite type stack. The following commutative diagram consists of excision sequences, with vertical maps given by the exterior product.</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="72.84" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-36.42px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 439.67 72.84" width="439.67"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,72.84) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(219.84,0) translate(0,36.42)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -219.83 -27.9)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 72.83)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(53.19,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -47.24 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.83em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="94.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(T)\otimes A_{*}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(T)\otimes A_{*}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 106.39 0) translate(86.95,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -47.79 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="95.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(Y)\otimes A_{*}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 247.08 0) translate(86.88,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -47.71 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="95.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(U)\otimes A_{*}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(U)\otimes A_{*}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 387.63 0) translate(42.63,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 64.31)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 13.75 0) translate(39.44,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -33.49 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.84em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="66.97"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A_{*}(T\times X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="f