<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">is <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{-1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s})\cap R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{-1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s})\cap R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 8.5. ‣ 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.5</span></a>, we see that <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{-1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s})\cap R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{-1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s})\cap R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that the closure of <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}(R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}(R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the closure of the image of <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{M}_{g,N}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{M}_{g,N}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> has finite fibers (corresponding to the ways of labeling all points in fibers with ramification) so the map <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\epsilon^{-1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s})\cap R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\epsilon^{-1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s})\cap R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> also has finite fibers. We assumed the map <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has generically finite fibers, so <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> must have generically finite fibers. Therefore,
the closure of <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}(R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}(R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an FPH cycle, so it is tautological (see Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 8.1. ‣ 8.1. Hurwitz spaces with marked specified ramification ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next we treat the case <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the intersection <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}\cap\epsilon^{-1}(T^{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}\cap\epsilon^{-1}(T^{a})</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> components: For each <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a component <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is ramified to order <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+2</annotation></semantics></math>. There is also a component <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in which the general cover has simple ramification at all <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (and another, unmarked point has ramification order <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+2</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2208.02357v2/Yi.svg" id="S8.SS2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="588" height="148"></object>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">For each component <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we want to find a map <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}(\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> whose image is dense in that component. Then, the rest of the proof will proceed as before.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{0}=(a+2,1,\ldots,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{0}=(a+2,1,\ldots,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{i}=(2,1,\ldots,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{i}=(2,1,\ldots,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,m:=2g-2+2k-a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,m:=2g-2+2k-a-1</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, let <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N:=\sum\ell(\mu^{i})=m(k-1)-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N:=\sum\ell(\mu^{i})=m(k-1)-a</annotation></semantics></math>.
We form <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}(\mu^{0},\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}(\mu^{0},\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})</annotation></semantics></math> which has a natural map to <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that our markings on <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}</annotation></semantics></math> are such that <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique ramification point in the <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-ramified fiber. Let <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the map that forgets all markings besides <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0},\ldots,p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0},\ldots,p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2208.02357v2/forget.svg" id="S8.SS2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="499" height="168"></object>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We realize the different components <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the image of</p>
<table id="S8.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}(\mu^{0},\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n+1}\xrightarrow{f_{i}}\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}(\mu^{0},\mu^{1},\ldots,\mu^{m})\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,N}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n+1}\xrightarrow{f_{i}}\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p14.2" class="ltx_p">under composition with different forgetful maps <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n+1}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n+1}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To obtain <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we should let <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> forget the marking <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. To obtain <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which forgets the marking <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and relabels <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the new <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\mathrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\mathrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> marking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{a,\circ}\subset T^{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∘</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{a,\circ}\subset T^{a}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open locus of covers with one point of ramification order exactly <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+2</annotation></semantics></math> and no other points of higher order ramification (this will replace the role of <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math> case).
By construction, the image of (<a href="#S8.E6" title="In Proof of Proposition . ‣ 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.6</span></a>) is <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{-1}(T^{a,\circ})\cap Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∘</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{-1}(T^{a,\circ})\cap Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 8.5. ‣ 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.5</span></a>, this is dense in <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the proof finishes as before, realizing the closure of each <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}(Y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}(Y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> as an FPH cycle. In particular, the closure of <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}(R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}\cap\epsilon^{-1}(T^{a}))=\beta_{n}(Y_{0})\cup\cdots\cup\beta_{n}(Y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}(R_{1}\cap\cdots\cap R_{n}\cap\epsilon^{-1}(T^{a}))=\beta_{n}(Y_{0})\cup\cdots\cup\beta_{n}(Y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a union of tautological cycles, hence tautological.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Trigonal curves</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\subseteq\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\subseteq\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the locus of curves of gonality at most <math id="S9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our goal in this section is to show that <math id="S9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and
that all classes supported on the locus <math id="S9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\subseteq\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\subseteq\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological when <math id="S9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small compared to <math id="S9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
To do so, we work with the Hurwitz space <math id="S9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing degree <math id="S9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> covers <math id="S9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct marked points.</p>
</div>
<section id="S9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.1. </span>Strategy</h3>

<div id="S9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can of course pullback tautological classes from <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> along</p>
<table id="S9.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In 9.1. Strategy ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>) were surjective, then by the push-pull formula and the fact that the fundamental class of <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological (by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Proposition 1]</cite>), we would be done.
When <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, our work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> shows (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In 9.1. Strategy ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>) is surjective for all <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, we shall see soon that (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In 9.1. Strategy ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>) is <em id="S9.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> surjective for any <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>!
Nevertheless, the push-pull formula allows us to reduce to understanding generators for <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <em id="S9.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">module</em> over the image of (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In 9.1. Strategy ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the next subsection, we give a construction of <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small. This gives rise to generators for
<math id="S9.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> that come from the algebra of structure theorems for degree <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> covers. Then we relate these generators to FPH cycles, studied in the previous section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.2. </span>Construction of the stack</h3>

<div id="S9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The basic idea is that, using structure theorems for degree <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> covers, the moduli space of smooth, genus <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> triple covers of <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as the moduli space of smooth curves of an appropriate class on a Hirzebruch surface. We constructed the latter in our earlier work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> as an open substack of a vector bundle over a moduli space of vector bundles on <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. To briefly review this construction, let <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be the moduli stack of rank <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, degree <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+2</annotation></semantics></math> globally generated vector bundles on <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles. (This <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the choice of Hirzebruch surface).
The stack <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> comes equipped with a universal <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> bundle <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and a universal rank <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> bundle <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. The projectivization <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\to\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\to\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is our universal Hirzebruch surface.
Our trigonal curves live inside <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> with class</p>
<table id="S9.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}:=\gamma^{*}\det\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(3)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}:=\gamma^{*}\det\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(3).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We then define the vector bundle <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}:=\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L})=\det\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{3}\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}:=\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L})=\det\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{3}\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the open substack where <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is globally generated on fibers of <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By cohomology and base change, <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=\pi_{*}\mathcal{U}|_{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=\pi_{*}\mathcal{U}|_{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle over <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fibers are equations of curves in the appropriate class on the corresponding Hirzebruch surface.
We prove in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Lemma 5.1]</cite> that <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the open substack of <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> where the vanishing of the equation is a smooth curve in each fiber over <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In a similar fashion, the moduli space <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth, genus <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> triple covers of <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points is the same as the moduli space of smooth curves of an appropriate class on a Hirzebruch surface with marked points.
To mark <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points on Hirzebruch surfaces, we consider <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}=\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\times_{\mathcal{B}}\cdots\times_{\mathcal{B}}\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}=\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\times_{\mathcal{B}}\cdots\times_{\mathcal{B}}\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then need to consider curves in the correct class that pass through the specificed points.
The key point is that when <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small, <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S9.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distinct</em> points on a smooth curve of our desired class <em id="S9.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">impose independent conditions</em> on the linear system for <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. This allows us to construct <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in a similar manner, as an open substack of a vector bundle over an open substack of <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}:(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}:(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection onto the <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> factor.
Let us continue the same notation as before, so we have a digram</p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="94.76" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-59.14px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 257.66 94.76" width="257.66"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,94.76) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(128.83,0) translate(0,59.14)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -128.83 -27.1)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 71.24)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.59)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 11.46 0) translate(17.14,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -11.18 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="22.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 57.19 0) translate(58.24,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -19.08 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.76em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="38.15"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 198.58 0) translate(44.36,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -5.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 62.72)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(28.6,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -22.64 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.27em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="45.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 62.78 0) translate(52.66,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.49 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.95em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="26.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 140.47 0) translate(43.88,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -4.71 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 195.01 0) translate(47.93,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -8.76 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.27em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="17.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{B}^{\prime}.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{B}^{\prime}.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -100.23 15.69 L -100.23 -10.8"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -100.23 -11.08)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -82.82 24.49 L -39.26 24.49"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -38.98 24.49)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -13.4 13 L -13.4 -10.8"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -13.4 -11.08)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 11.91 24.49 L 102.13 24.49"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 102.41 24.49)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 114.11 15.69 L 114.11 -10.8"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 114.11 -11.08)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -71.36 -34.14 C -8 -55.1 37.1 -53.77 98.6 -29.3"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.92915 0.36969 -0.36969 0.92915 98.85 -29.2)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.08 -56.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.33em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="4.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -71.36 -23.64 L -33.67 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -33.4 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -57.28 -18.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.73em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="10.08"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">η</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.33 -23.64 L 44.02 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 44.29 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 22.54 -18.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.81"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 66.46 -23.64 L 98.56 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 98.83 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 79.55 -20.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.SS2.p3.pic1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 66.46 -23.64 L 98.56 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 98.83 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">From the definition of <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a natural evaluation map on <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}=\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\pi_{*}\gamma_{*}\mathcal{L}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}=\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\pi_{*}\gamma_{*}\mathcal{L}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We can then take the direct sum of the pullbacks of these evaluation maps</p>
<table id="S9.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\eta_{i}^{*}\mathcal{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\eta_{i}^{*}\mathcal{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\subset\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\subset\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the preimage of the zero section under (<a href="#S9.E2" title="In 9.2. Construction of the stack ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a>).
The stack <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes tuples <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle on <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vanishing of a section of <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\det E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\det E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\in C</annotation></semantics></math> is a collection of <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (not necessarily distinct) points.
The Hurwitz space <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes the same kinds of tuples but where <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of dimension <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct.
As such, there is a natural open inclusion of <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that
the stack <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S9.SS2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> a vector bundle over <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> because its fiber dimension jumps when the collection of points does not impose independent conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In order to gain a better understanding of <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and from it <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we want to know when the map (<a href="#S9.E2" title="In 9.2. Construction of the stack ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a>) is surjective. By cohomology and base change, we can reduce to checking surjectivity on the fibers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.1</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be a rank <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle on <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points. Suppose there exists a smooth curve <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in class <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L:=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the evaluation map</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},L)\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,L|_{\Gamma})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},L)\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,L|_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is surjective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The evaluation map factors as</span></p>
<table id="S9.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S9.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},L)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rightarrow H^{0}(C,L|_{C})\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,L|_{\Gamma})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rightarrow H^{0}(C,L|_{C})\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,L|_{\Gamma}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">The first map is surjective because <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the construction of <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to the relative canonical of <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is
<math id="S9.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(1)|_{C}\cong\omega_{C}\otimes\gamma^{*}\omega_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(1)|_{C}\cong\omega_{C}\otimes\gamma^{*}\omega_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Theorem 2.1(2)]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Example 3.12]</cite>).
In particular,</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg L=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)|_{C})+\deg(\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee})|_{C}=3(2g-2+2\cdot 3)-(g+2)\cdot 3=3g+6." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg L=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)|_{C})+\deg(\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee})|_{C}=3(2g-2+2\cdot 3)-(g+2)\cdot 3=3g+6.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C,L)=\chi(C,L)=2g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C,L)=\chi(C,L)=2g+7</annotation></semantics></math>. Now consider the exact sequence on <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow L(-\Gamma)\rightarrow L\rightarrow L|_{\Gamma}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow L(-\Gamma)\rightarrow L\rightarrow L|_{\Gamma}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C,L)\to H^{0}(\Gamma,L|_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C,L)\to H^{0}(\Gamma,L|_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> will be surjective if <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(C,L(-\Gamma))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(C,L(-\Gamma))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Serre duality,
<math id="S9.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(C,L(-\Gamma))=H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes L^{\vee}(\Gamma))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(C,L(-\Gamma))=H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes L^{\vee}(\Gamma))</annotation></semantics></math>, which vanishes so long as</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&gt;\deg(\omega_{C}\otimes L^{\vee}(\Gamma))=2g-2-(3g+6)+n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&gt;\deg(\omega_{C}\otimes L^{\vee}(\Gamma))=2g-2-(3g+6)+n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">or equivalently, so long as <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now, define <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the locus over which the evaluation map (<a href="#S9.E2" title="In 9.2. Construction of the stack ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a>) is surjective. We know that <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is open, but a priori it could be empty, and in fact <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> will be empty when <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is too large.
However, when <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math>,
Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 9.1. ‣ 9.2. Construction of the stack ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a> shows that the image of <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, we find that the inclusion <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, by definition of <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the kernel of the restriction of (<a href="#S9.E2" title="In 9.2. Construction of the stack ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a>) to <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle over <math id="S9.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.2</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the CKgP for <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have maps</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}\to U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\to\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}\to U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\to\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">where each <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subset</annotation></semantics></math> is an open inclusion and each arrow is a vector bundle or product of projective bundles. Using Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 3.7. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>, it thus suffices to show that <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP. But <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient of an open subset of affine space by <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g+2}\times\GL_{g+4}\times\BSL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>GL</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BSL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g+2}\times\GL_{g+4}\times\BSL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Proposition 4.2]</cite>). Equivalently <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open inside a vector bundle over <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BGL_{g+2}\times\BGL_{g+4}\times\BSL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BSL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BGL_{g+2}\times\BGL_{g+4}\times\BSL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we are done because <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BGL_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BGL_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BSL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BSL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BSL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have the CKgP (Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.3. </span>Generators for the Chow ring</h3>

<div id="S9.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our construction of <math id="S9.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to generators for its Chow ring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.3</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is a surjection <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By excision, we have a series of surjections</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\rightarrow A^{*}(U)\cong A^{*}(\mathcal{Y}|_{U})\rightarrow A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\rightarrow A^{*}(U)\cong A^{*}(\mathcal{Y}|_{U})\rightarrow A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The middle map is an isomorphism because <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle over <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}\gamma^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}\gamma^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The classes
<math id="S9.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},\ldots,z_{n},\zeta_{1},\ldots,z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},\ldots,z_{n},\zeta_{1},\ldots,z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> generate <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> as an <em id="S9.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">algebra</em> over <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The projective bundle theorem gives us relations</p>
<table id="S9.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}^{2}+c_{1}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})\zeta_{i}+c_{2}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})=0\qquad\text{and}\qquad z_{i}^{2}+c_{2}(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}^{2}+c_{1}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})\zeta_{i}+c_{2}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})=0\qquad\text{and}\qquad z_{i}^{2}+c_{2}(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Taking into account these relations, we see <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated
as a <em id="S9.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">module</em> over <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> by monomials of the form</p>
<table id="S9.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{1}^{a_{1}}z_{2}^{a_{2}}\cdots z_{n}^{a_{n}}\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}\qquad a_{i},b_{i}\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}^{a_{1}}z_{2}^{a_{2}}\cdots z_{n}^{a_{n}}\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}\qquad a_{i},b_{i}\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Combining this with Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 9.3. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a>, we see that the classes in (<a href="#S9.E4" title="In 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.4</span></a>) generate <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as a module over the image of <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile,
the map <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{H}_{3,g}\to\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{H}_{3,g}\to\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. We proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Theorem 1.1(1)]</cite> that <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g})</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective and that for <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the image is generated by the pullback of <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the image of <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is just the subring generated by <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\kappa}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\kappa}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the pullback of <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Note that the tautological rings of <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are closed under pullbacks, so <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\kappa}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\kappa}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pullback of a tautological class on <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.)
Hence, we obtain:</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.4</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math>.
The classes in (<a href="#S9.E4" title="In 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.4</span></a>) generate
<math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as a module over the subring generated by <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\kappa}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\kappa}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our task now is to relate <math id="S9.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to some more geometrically defined classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.5</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}=\zeta_{i}-2z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}=\zeta_{i}-2z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to treat the one pointed case.
Because <math id="S9.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal curve <math id="S9.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{H}_{3,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{H}_{3,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that <math id="S9.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=c_{1}(\omega_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=c_{1}(\omega_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>. The result now follows from the first equation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Example 3.12]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Using Lemmas <a href="#S9.E3" title="In 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a> and <a href="#S9.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 9.5. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.5</span></a> together with the relations in (<a href="#S9.E3" title="In 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a>), we obtain:</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.6</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math>. As a module over the subring generated by <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\kappa}_{1},\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\kappa}_{1},\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the monomials <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, our goal is to show that these monomials <math id="S9.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> push forward to tautological classes on <math id="S9.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. To do so, we use some geometrically defined cycles that represent these monomials.
Recall the divisors</p>
<table id="S9.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}=\{(C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}):p_{i}\text{ is a ramification point of $C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}$}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> is a ramification point of </mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}=\{(C,p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}):p_{i}\text{ is a ramification point of $C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}$}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">introduced in Section <a href="#S8" title="8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
The first step is to identify the fundamental class of <math id="S9.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of our generators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.7</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R_{i}]=\zeta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R_{i}]=\zeta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}:\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}:\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is the composition of the <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th section <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}:\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}:\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> with the universal embedding <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\mathcal{C}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\mathcal{C}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction of the embedding, <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1)\cong\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1)\cong\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal degree <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> map <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Theorem 2.1]</cite>.
Thus, <math id="S9.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}=\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))=\sigma_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}=\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))=\sigma_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the result follows from (<a href="#S8.E5" title="In 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.5</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}:\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}:\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the forgetful map. We now show that our module generators for <math id="S9.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S9.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> push forward to tautological classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.8</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\ldots,i_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\ldots,i_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be a subset of distinct indices in <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{n*}(\zeta_{i_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{i_{j}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n*}(\zeta_{i_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{i_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological in <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 9.7. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.7</span></a>, we have
<math id="S9.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{i_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{i_{j}}=[R_{i_{1}}]\cdots[R_{i_{j}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{i_{j}}=[R_{i_{1}}]\cdots[R_{i_{j}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, so the result follows from Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 8.4. ‣ 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We now conclude that the trigonal locus has our desired properties, when the number of marked points is sufficiently small.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.9</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S9.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq g+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq g+7</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S9.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and all classes supported on <math id="S9.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 6.1 (Corollary 1.1 of []). ‣ 6. Genus 2 and hyperelliptic curves in general ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, we know that when <math id="S9.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 2g+6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 2g+6</annotation></semantics></math> all classes supported on <math id="S9.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological and <math id="S9.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, so it remains to show the same for <math id="S9.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The map <math id="S9.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and surjective.
Combining Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 9.2. ‣ 9.2. Construction of the stack ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 3.10. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>, we see that <math id="S9.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP. Applying Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, the union <math id="S9.SS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}=(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2})\cup\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}=(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2})\cup\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Meanwhile, the map <math id="S9.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
induces a surjection on Chow groups with rational coefficients. In particular, every class supported on <math id="S9.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}\subset\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}\subset\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the image of <math id="S9.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{n})_{*}:A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{n})_{*}:A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{3,g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, it suffices to show that the image of <math id="S9.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{n})_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{n})_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.
By Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 9.6. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.6</span></a> and the push-pull formula, we are reduced to showing that <math id="S9.SS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\beta_{n})_{*}(\zeta_{i_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{i_{j}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{n})_{*}(\zeta_{i_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{i_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for any subset of distinct indices <math id="S9.SS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\ldots,i_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\ldots,i_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is proved in Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 9.8. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.8</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 9.10</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can adapt the arguments of this section to work in the case <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
We would then have two separate proofs showing that classes supported on <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{3,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,n}^{3}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{3,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological, but they seem to work for different values of <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The first, Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm5" title="Corollary 7.5. ‣ 7.2. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 7. Plane curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a> using plane curves, gives <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second, Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 9.9. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.9</span></a> using the Hurwitz space, gives <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>. This discrepancy is actually a mirage: every <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> on a non-hyperelliptic curve <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> comes from projecting from a point <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in C</annotation></semantics></math> in the canonical embedding. Therefore, the Hurwitz space <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> really corresponds to <math id="S9.Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{3}_{3,1}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}^{2}_{3,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{3}_{3,1}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}^{2}_{3,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which explains why the plane model appears to give one more marked point than the Hurwitz space model.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">In genus <math id="S9.SS3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S9.SS3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{4,n}=\mathcal{M}_{4,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{4,n}=\mathcal{M}_{4,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, so Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 9.9. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.9</span></a> gives the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.11</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S9.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S9.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{4,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{4,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S9.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}=R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}=R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10. </span>Tetragonal curves</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will attempt to show that classes supported on <math id="S10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological. We do not succeed to show this in full for arbitrary <math id="S10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, it is not true in general that all classes supported on <math id="S10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. But the argument will suffice for the low genus cases treated this paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.1. </span>Strategy</h3>

<div id="S10.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The strategy is quite similar to the trigonal case, but with a few extra complications. We first define a subring of <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> that deserves to be called the tautological ring. By relating the generators to geometrically defined classes we show that these tautological classes on <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> push forward to tautological classes on <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is not generated by tautological classes in general,
we find a large open substack <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by tautological classes. The next task is to understand the complement of <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. In general, there may be <em id="S10.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-tautological</em> classes coming from this complement. In particular, the bielliptic locus in genus <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the complement. For the genera of interest in this paper, however, we will be able to give a complete description of the complement and show that any Chow classes coming from it are indeed tautological. For example, in genus <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>, the complement consists of pointed hyperelliptic, trigonal, and plane quintic curves, but we know from previous sections that any classes supported on these loci are tautological.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.2. </span>Tautological classes</h3>

<div id="S10.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using structure theorems for degree <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> covers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, the moduli space of degree <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as the moduli space of codimension <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> complete intersections in a certain class on a <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the moduli stack of pairs of globally generated vector bundles of rank <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and degree <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+3</annotation></semantics></math> and rank <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and degree <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+3</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> together with an isomorphism <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det\mathcal{E}\cong\det\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det\mathcal{E}\cong\det\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, as constructed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Definition 5.2]</cite>. There is a universal <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>- bundle <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{P}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{P}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, a universal rank <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+3</annotation></semantics></math> bundle <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a universal rank <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+3</annotation></semantics></math> bundle <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\rightarrow\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}\rightarrow\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> bundle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is a natural morphism <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}\to\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}\to\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> that sends a degree <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cover to its associated vector bundles on <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem defines an embedding of the universal curve <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> into the projectivization of the pullback of <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Hence, we obtain a diagram</p>
<table id="S10.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
