<p id="S10.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, we studied the map <math id="S10.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math>; we called the image the tautological subring for <math id="S10.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and related it to the tautological ring on <math id="S10.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pointed version of this is to study the image of <math id="S10.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which similarly deserves to be called the tautological subring for <math id="S10.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The key result we need is Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 10.5. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.5</span></a> below, which relates tautological classes on <math id="S10.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to tautological classes on <math id="S10.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The first step is to describe the image of <math id="S10.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> in terms the classes of ramification loci <math id="S10.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{a}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S8.E3" title="In 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.1</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The image of <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as a module over <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,[T^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,[T^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The pullback map <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> factors through</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">The image of <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math> is the tautological subring <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Theorem 3.10]</cite>.
Next, we claim that <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as a module over <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> by the classes of the ramification loci <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,T^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,T^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We proved something similar to this in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Lemma 7.9]</cite>, where <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
Here, we want to use a slightly different basis. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Lemma 7.9(1)]</cite>, we know <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1},[T^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1},[T^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}[T^{1}],\kappa_{1}^{2},\kappa_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}[T^{1}],\kappa_{1}^{2},\kappa_{2},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Equation 7.5
and Lemmas 7.6 and 7.7]</cite>, we can write each of these classes in terms of the usual spanning set <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}^{2},a_{2}^{\prime}a_{1},a_{2}^{\prime 2},a_{3}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}^{2},a_{2}^{\prime}a_{1},a_{2}^{\prime 2},a_{3}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Corollary 5.6(2)]</cite>. One then checks that the change of basis matrix is full rank for all <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. It has determinant</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1658880g-2985984}{g^{2}+4g+3}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>1658880</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2985984</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1658880g-2985984}{g^{2}+4g+3},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">which never vanishes for <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> an integer. From <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Lemma 7.9(3)]</cite> it is clear <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}^{2}[T^{1}],\kappa_{1}^{3},\kappa_{1}[T^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}^{2}[T^{1}],\kappa_{1}^{3},\kappa_{1}[T^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> also form a spanning set of <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{3}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{3}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Lemma 7.9(4) and (5)]</cite> complete the proof that <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,[T^{1}],[T^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,[T^{1}],[T^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> generate <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math> as a module over <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The next step is to study the generators for <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}))</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}:(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\to\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}:(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\to\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th projection map.
Let <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}\gamma^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}\gamma^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}:=\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The classes
<math id="S10.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},\ldots,z_{n},\zeta_{1},\ldots,\zeta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},\ldots,z_{n},\zeta_{1},\ldots,\zeta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> generate <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> as an <em id="S10.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">algebra</em> over <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}=c_{2}(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))\in A^{*}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}=c_{2}(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1))\in A^{*}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The projective bundle theorem gives us relations</p>
<table id="S10.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}^{3}+c_{1}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})\zeta_{i}^{2}+c_{2}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})\zeta_{i}+c_{3}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})=0\qquad\text{and}\qquad z_{i}^{2}+c_{2}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}^{3}+c_{1}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})\zeta_{i}^{2}+c_{2}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})\zeta_{i}+c_{3}(\mathcal{E}^{\vee})=0\qquad\text{and}\qquad z_{i}^{2}+c_{2}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Taking into account these relations, we see <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated
as a <em id="S10.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">module</em> over <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> by monomials of the form</p>
<table id="S10.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{1}^{a_{1}}z_{2}^{a_{2}}\cdots z_{n}^{a_{n}}\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}\qquad a_{i}\leq 1\qquad b_{i}\leq 2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}^{a_{1}}z_{2}^{a_{2}}\cdots z_{n}^{a_{n}}\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\zeta_{2}^{b_{2}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}\qquad a_{i}\leq 1\qquad b_{i}\leq 2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now we relate <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and ramification classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.2</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have</span></p>
<ol id="S10.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S10.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S10.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}=\psi_{i}+2z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}=\psi_{i}+2z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}^{2}=\psi_{i}^{2}+2\zeta_{i}\psi_{i}-4c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}^{2}=\psi_{i}^{2}+2\zeta_{i}\psi_{i}-4c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first statement is proved exactly the same way as Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 9.5. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.5</span></a>.
For the second, we simply square the equality in (1).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.3</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R_{i}]=\zeta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R_{i}]=\zeta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Letting <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be the map in (<a href="#S10.E2" title="In 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>), we have <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1)=\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1)=\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal degree <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> map. Hence, <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))=\sigma_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(1))=\sigma_{i}^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> so the result follows from (<a href="#S8.E5" title="In 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.5</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to give generators for tautological classes on <math id="S10.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.4</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following classes generate the image of <math id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as a module over <math id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S10.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[R_{i_{1}}]\cdots[R_{i_{j}}]\cdot[T^{c}]\qquad i_{1},\ldots,i_{j}\text{ distinct},\qquad c\leq 2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> distinct</mtext><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R_{i_{1}}]\cdots[R_{i_{j}}]\cdot[T^{c}]\qquad i_{1},\ldots,i_{j}\text{ distinct},\qquad c\leq 2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}\subset A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}\subset A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> be the graded subring generated by <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the image of <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 10.1. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>, the ring <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as a module over <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{c}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{c}]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 10.3. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a>, we have <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R_{i}]=\zeta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R_{i}]=\zeta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It therefore suffices to show that the monomials <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> generate <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as a module over <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To accomplish this, we show that each monomial of the form in (<a href="#S10.E5" title="In 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.5</span></a>) lies in the span of elements of <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> times monomials of the form <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS2.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
First note that by (<a href="#S10.E4" title="In 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.4</span></a>), we have</span></p>
<table id="S10.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\zeta_{i}^{3}-(a_{1}+(g+3)a_{2}^{\prime}z_{i})\zeta_{i}^{2}+(a_{2}+a_{2}^{\prime}z_{i})\zeta-(a_{3}+a_{3}^{\prime}z_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\zeta_{i}^{3}-(a_{1}+(g+3)a_{2}^{\prime}z_{i})\zeta_{i}^{2}+(a_{2}+a_{2}^{\prime}z_{i})\zeta-(a_{3}+a_{3}^{\prime}z_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody><tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="5">where <math id="S10.EGx5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i},a_{i}^{\prime}\in A^{*}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i},a_{i}^{\prime}\in A^{*}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, and so will also be viewed as elements of <math id="S10.EGx5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 10.2. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, we can substitute in <math id="S10.EGx5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}=\frac{1}{2}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}=\frac{1}{2}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to get</td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S10.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\zeta_{i}^{3}-(a_{1}+\tfrac{1}{2}(g+3)(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i}))\zeta_{i}^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}(a_{2}+a_{2}^{\prime}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i}))\zeta_{i}-(a_{3}+a_{3}^{\prime}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\zeta_{i}^{3}-(a_{1}+\tfrac{1}{2}(g+3)(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i}))\zeta_{i}^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}(a_{2}+a_{2}^{\prime}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i}))\zeta_{i}-(a_{3}+a_{3}^{\prime}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody><tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="5">Because the coefficient <math id="S10.EGx5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\frac{1}{2}(g+3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\frac{1}{2}(g+3)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.EGx5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> above is non-zero, we may solve to find</td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S10.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\zeta_{i}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\zeta_{i}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in S^{1}\cdot\zeta_{i}^{2}+S^{2}\cdot\zeta_{i}+S^{3}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in S^{1}\cdot\zeta_{i}^{2}+S^{2}\cdot\zeta_{i}+S^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now,
suppose we are given a monomial of the form in (<a href="#S10.E5" title="In 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.5</span></a>). Using Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 10.2. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>(1), we can replace each <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(\zeta_{i}-\psi_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. This will give us a sum of terms of the form <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S^{*}\cdot\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}^{\prime}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}\cdot\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}^{\prime}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{\prime}\leq b_{i}+a_{i}\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{\prime}\leq b_{i}+a_{i}\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then use (<a href="#S10.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.7</span></a>) to eliminate any terms with a <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain a sum of terms of the form <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S^{*}\cdot\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}^{\prime\prime}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}\cdot\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}^{\prime\prime}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{\prime\prime}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{\prime\prime}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we can use Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 10.2. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>(2) to eliminate each <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
that appears, thus leaving a sum of terms of the form <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S^{*}\cdot\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}^{\prime\prime\prime}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}^{\prime\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mo mathsize="1.400em">′′′</mo></msubsup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo mathsize="1.400em">′′′</mo></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}\cdot\zeta_{1}^{b_{1}^{\prime\prime\prime}}\cdots\zeta_{n}^{b_{n}^{\prime\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{\prime\prime\prime}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{\prime\prime\prime}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove our desired result, which roughly says tautological classes on <math id="S10.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> push forward to tautological classes on <math id="S10.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.5</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let
<math id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}^{\prime}:\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\smallsetminus\beta_{n}^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3})\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}^{\prime}:\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\smallsetminus\beta_{n}^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3})\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in (<a href="#S8.E2" title="In 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>). Suppose that <math id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\smallsetminus\beta_{n}^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\smallsetminus\beta_{n}^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}))</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the image of</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\smallsetminus\beta_{n}^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\smallsetminus\beta_{n}^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{n*}^{\prime}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n*}^{\prime}x</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological on <math id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using the push-pull formula, it suffices to show that each of the generators in (<a href="#S10.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 10.4. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.4</span></a>) pushes forward to a tautological class on <math id="S10.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 8.4. ‣ 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Our next task is to find a large open substack <math id="S10.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> whose Chow ring is generated by tautological classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.3. </span>Construction of a certain substack</h3>

<div id="S10.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The tetragonal curves live inside <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> as the vanishing loci of sections of the rank <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> bundle <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}:=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}\mathcal{F}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}:=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}\mathcal{F}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\gamma_{*}(\mathcal{W})=\Sym^{2}\mathcal{E}\otimes\mathcal{F}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\gamma_{*}(\mathcal{W})=\Sym^{2}\mathcal{E}\otimes\mathcal{F}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complement of the support of <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. By cohomology and base change, <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=\pi_{*}\mathcal{U}|_{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=\pi_{*}\mathcal{U}|_{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle over <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fibers correspond to equations of tetragonal curves. Define <math id="S10.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g}\subset\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g}\subset\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the open substack representing smooth tetragonal curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We construct the moduli space <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> similarly. Throughout this section, we shall work over <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, so when we write <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>, we mean <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}|_{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}|_{\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}|_{\pi^{-1}\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}|_{\pi^{-1}\mathcal{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}:(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}:(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection to the <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> factor, so we have a diagram:</p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="94.76" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-59.14px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 257.66 94.76" width="257.66"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,94.76) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(128.83,0) translate(0,59.14)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -128.83 -27.1)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 71.24)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.59)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 11.46 0) translate(17.14,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -11.18 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="22.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 57.19 0) translate(58.24,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -19.08 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.76em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="38.15"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 198.58 0) translate(44.36,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -5.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 62.72)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(28.6,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -22.64 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.27em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="45.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 62.78 0) translate(52.66,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.49 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.95em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="26.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 140.47 0) translate(43.88,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -4.71 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 195.01 0) translate(47.93,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -8.76 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.27em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="17.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{B}^{\prime}.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{B}^{\prime}.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -82.82 24.49 L -39.26 24.49"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -38.98 24.49)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -100.23 15.69 L -100.23 -10.8"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -100.23 -11.08)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -13.4 13 L -13.4 -10.8"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -13.4 -11.08)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 11.91 24.49 L 102.13 24.49"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 102.41 24.49)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 114.11 15.69 L 114.11 -10.8"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 114.11 -11.08)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -71.36 -23.64 L -33.67 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -33.4 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -57.28 -18.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.73em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="10.08"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">η</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -71.36 -34.14 C -8 -55.1 37.1 -53.77 98.6 -29.3"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.92915 0.36969 -0.36969 0.92915 98.85 -29.2)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.08 -56.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.33em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="4.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.33 -23.64 L 44.02 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 44.29 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 22.54 -18.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.81"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 66.46 -23.64 L 98.56 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 98.83 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 79.55 -20.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S10.SS3.p2.pic1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 66.46 -23.64 L 98.56 -23.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 98.83 -23.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S10.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We have evaluation maps
in a rank <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> bundle</p>
<table id="S10.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{W}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\pi^{*}\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">on <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Pulling back to <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and taking a sum over the factors, we obtain an evaluation map on <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S10.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\eta_{i}^{*}\mathcal{W}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\eta_{i}^{*}\mathcal{W}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\subset\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\subset\epsilon^{*}\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the preimage of the zero section under (<a href="#S10.E9" title="In 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a>). The stack <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes tuples <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,F,C,p_{1},\dots,p_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,F,C,p_{1},\dots,p_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vanishing locus of a section of <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> which contains <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an open inclusion <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the open conditions that <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and <math id="S10.SS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We want to know when (<a href="#S10.E9" title="In 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a>) is surjective. By cohomology and base change, we reduce to the case of a single curve <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a complete intersection on <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> in class <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}F^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}F^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.6</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\mathcal{O}(e_{1})\oplus\mathcal{O}(e_{2})\oplus\mathcal{O}(e_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\mathcal{O}(e_{1})\oplus\mathcal{O}(e_{2})\oplus\mathcal{O}(e_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\mathcal{O}(f_{1})\oplus\mathcal{O}(f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\mathcal{O}(f_{1})\oplus\mathcal{O}(f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\leq f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\leq f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be vector bundles of degree <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+3</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4f_{1}-2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4f_{1}-2g+1</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points. Suppose that there exists a smooth, irreducible curve <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that is the zero locus of a section of <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W:=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}F^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W:=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}F^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the evaluation map</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},W)\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,W|_{\Gamma})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},W)\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,W|_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is surjective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We factor the evaluation map through</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},W)\rightarrow H^{0}(C,W|_{C})\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,W|_{\Gamma})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},W)\rightarrow H^{0}(C,W|_{C})\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,W|_{\Gamma}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The first map is surjective because <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Theorem 2.1(2)]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Example 3.12]</cite>, <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{C}\otimes\gamma^{*}\omega_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{C}\otimes\gamma^{*}\omega_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the exact sequence on <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow W|_{C}(-\Gamma)\rightarrow W\rightarrow W|_{\Gamma}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow W|_{C}(-\Gamma)\rightarrow W\rightarrow W|_{\Gamma}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">The map <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C,W)\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,W|_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C,W)\rightarrow H^{0}(\Gamma,W|_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective if <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(C,W|_{C}(-\Gamma))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(C,W|_{C}(-\Gamma))=0</annotation></semantics></math>. By Serre duality,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(C,W|_{C}(-\Gamma))=H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma))^{\vee}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(C,W|_{C}(-\Gamma))=H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma))^{\vee}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f_{i}}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}\mathcal{O}(f_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f_{i}}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}\mathcal{O}(f_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W_{f_{1}}\oplus W_{f_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W_{f_{1}}\oplus W_{f_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg W_{f_{i}}|_{C}=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)|_{C}\otimes\gamma^{*}\mathcal{O}(-f_{i})|_{C})=2(2g-2+4\cdot 2)-4f_{i}=4g+12-4f_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg W_{f_{i}}|_{C}=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)|_{C}\otimes\gamma^{*}\mathcal{O}(-f_{i})|_{C})=2(2g-2+4\cdot 2)-4f_{i}=4g+12-4f_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">Then</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma))=H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W_{f_{1}}|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma))\oplus H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W_{f_{2}}|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma))=H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W_{f_{1}}|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma))\oplus H^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes W_{f_{2}}|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the required vanishing will occur if</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&gt;\deg\omega_{C}\otimes W_{f_{i}}|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma)=2g-2-(4g+12-4f_{i})+n=4f_{i}+n-2g-14," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&gt;\deg\omega_{C}\otimes W_{f_{i}}|_{C}^{\vee}(\Gamma)=2g-2-(4g+12-4f_{i})+n=4f_{i}+n-2g-14,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p4.7.1" class="ltx_text">equivalently if, <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 2g+13-4f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 2g+13-4f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}+f_{2}=g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}+f_{2}=g+3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\leq f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\leq f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can rewrite this as
<math id="S10.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 2g+13-4(g+3-f_{1})=4f_{1}-2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 2g+13-4(g+3-f_{1})=4f_{1}-2g+1</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 10.7</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the degree <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> case, our curve was defined by the vanishing of a section of a line bundle. In degree <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the zero locus of a section of a rank <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=F^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=F^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the splitting type of the rank <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> bundle <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> enters into the calculation of how many points we can mark and still know we are imposing independent conditions.
Observe also that <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\leq\frac{g+3}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\leq\frac{g+3}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the number of marked points we can hope to get with this technique is bounded (independent of <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>) via <math id="S10.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4f_{1}-2g+1\leq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4f_{1}-2g+1\leq 7</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It turns out that the locus of covers for which <math id="S10.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is small often corresponds curves with special geometry properties (see Lemmas <a href="#S10.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 10.9. ‣ 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a> and <a href="#S10.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 10.10. ‣ 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.10</span></a> below). One might hope to access these special curves by other means and then focus on their complement, the locus of covers with <math id="S10.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\geq f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\geq f</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. We therefore make the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10.8</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of Casnati–Ekedahl strata where <math id="S10.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\geq f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\geq f</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, <math id="S10.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}=\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{\prime}\smallsetminus\Supp R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathcal{F}(f-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}=\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{\prime}\smallsetminus\Supp R^{1}\pi_{*}\mathcal{F}(f-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that we write <math id="S10.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for the map to the moduli space of curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.9</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Section 4.3]</cite>, the complement of <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,5}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,5}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{5}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{5}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is also the locus where <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, if <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=3</annotation></semantics></math>, then we claim <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is trigonal. First, note that <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Section 4.3]</cite> is actually empty because <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}&gt;2e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}&gt;2e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which forces such curves to be singular by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Equation 4.7]</cite>. Therefore, if <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> has
splitting type <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,5)</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> has splitting type <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3,3)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case,
the image of <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cone over <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> passes through the cone point because <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{11}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{11}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg q_{11}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg q_{11}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Projecting from the cone point sends <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to a curve of bidegree <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,3)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we see that <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is trigonal.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">There is also a nice geometric explanation of the locus where <math id="S10.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is small in genus <math id="S10.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{n}\subset\mathcal{M}_{6,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{n}\subset\mathcal{M}_{6,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the preimage of the locus <math id="S10.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ\subset\mathcal{M}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ\subset\mathcal{M}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth plane quintics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.10</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The allowed pairs of splitting types were determined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Section 4.3]</cite>.
The claim follows immediately from Lemma 4.5 and Remark 4.7 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, for our purposes in genus <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> at least, it will be enough to know information about <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
To gain a better understanding of <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the locus over which the evaluation map (<a href="#S10.E9" title="In 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a>) is surjective. We know that <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is open, but a priori it could be empty, and in fact <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> will be empty when <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is too large.
However, when <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4f-2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4f-2g+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 9.1. ‣ 9.2. Construction of the stack ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a> shows that the image of <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, we find that the inclusion <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, by definition of <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the kernel of the restriction of (<a href="#S10.E9" title="In 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a>) to <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle over <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. In summary, there are maps</p>
<table id="S10.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}\rightarrow U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow\mathcal{P}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}\subset\mathcal{Y}|_{U}\rightarrow U\subset(\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow\mathcal{P}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}^{\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subset</annotation></semantics></math>’s are open inclusions and the arrows are vector bundles or projective bundles. This leads to the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.11</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S10.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4f-2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4f-2g+1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Considering (<a href="#S10.E10" title="In 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.10</span></a>) and our standard lemmas about the CKgP, it suffices to show that <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP. By definition, <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open inside a line bundle over <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{3,g+3}\times_{\BSL_{2}}\mathcal{V}_{2,g+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BSL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{3,g+3}\times_{\BSL_{2}}\mathcal{V}_{2,g+2}</annotation></semantics></math>
(defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Equation 4.1]</cite>), so by Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> it suffices to show
<math id="S10.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{3,g+3}\times_{\BSL_{2}}\mathcal{V}_{2,g+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>BSL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{3,g+3}\times_{\BSL_{2}}\mathcal{V}_{2,g+2}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP. Then, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Equation 4.1]</cite> realizes this stack as a quotient of an open subset of affine space by a product of <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> once more, together with Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>
completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.4. </span>Generators for the Chow ring</h3>

<div id="S10.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Just like in the degree <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> case, our demonstration that <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g,n}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP also gives rise to natural generators for its Chow ring. In particular, they are all tautological in the sense discussed in Section <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.12</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S10.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4f-2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4f-2g+1</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S10.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By excision, we have a series of surjections</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow A^{*}(U)\cong A^{*}(\mathcal{Y}|_{U})\rightarrow A^{*}(\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}((\mathbb{P}\mathcal{E}^{\vee})^{n}\rightarrow A^{*}(U)\cong A^{*}(\mathcal{Y}|_{U})\rightarrow A^{*}(\mathcal{H}^{f}_{4,g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The middle map is an isomorphism because <math id="S10.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle over <math id="S10.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.5. </span>Conclusion when <math id="S10.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S10.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In genus <math id="S10.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, we
use push forward along the proper map</p>
<table id="S10.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}_{n}:\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3})\to\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}_{n}:\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3})\to\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">(The equality of domains above is Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 10.9. ‣ 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a>.)
When <math id="S10.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S10.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4f-2g+1=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4f-2g+1=7</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.13</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S10.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S10.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}=R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}=R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We already know from Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 9.9. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.9</span></a>, that <math id="S10.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and that all classes supported on it are tautological. It thus remains to show that <math id="S10.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S10.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}=R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}=R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have a proper surjective map <math id="S10.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n}^{\prime}:\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}\to\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n}^{\prime}:\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}\to\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. We know <math id="S10.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,5,n}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP (Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm11" title="Lemma 10.11. ‣ 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.11</span></a>), so <math id="S10.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 3.10. ‣ 3.1. Basic lemmas ‣ 3. The Chow–Künneth generation property ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>.
We also know that <math id="S10.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{n*}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n*}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a surjection on Chow groups, so it suffices to show that the image of <math id="S10.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{n*}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n*}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.
The result now follows from Lemmas <a href="#S10.Thmthm12" title="Lemma 10.12. ‣ 10.4. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.12</span></a> and <a href="#S10.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 10.5. ‣ 10.2. Tautological classes ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.6. </span>Conclusion when <math id="S10.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S10.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S10.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math>, we use push forwards along the proper map</p>
<table id="S10.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}:\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})\to\mathcal{M}_{6,n}\smallsetminus(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}:\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}^{4}=\mathcal{H}_{4,6,n}\smallsetminus\beta^{-1}(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})\to\mathcal{M}_{6,n}\smallsetminus(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S10.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the plane quintic locus, defined as the image of <math id="S10.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{5,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{6,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{5,n}\to\mathcal{M}_{6,n}</annotation></semantics></math> from Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Plane curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
(The equality of domains above is Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 10.9. ‣ 10.3. Construction of a certain substack ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a>.)
When <math id="S10.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S10.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4f-2g+1=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4f-2g+1=5</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.14</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{6,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{6,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}=R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}=R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We already know from Lemma <a href="#S9.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 9.9. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.9</span></a>, that <math id="S10.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{5,n}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and that all classes supported on it are tautological.
The fundamental class of <math id="S10.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pullback of the class of the plane quintic locus on <math id="S10.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is tautological by an argument of Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> (as applied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>).
By Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 7.4. ‣ 7.2. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 7. Plane curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>, we know that <math id="S10.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(PQ_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(PQ_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by restrictions of tautological classes. By the push-pull formula, all classes supported on <math id="S10.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological. Furthermore, <math id="S10.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP by Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7.1. Construction of the stack ‣ 7. Plane curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">It thus remains to show that the stratum <math id="S10.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{6,n}\smallsetminus(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{6,n}\smallsetminus(\mathcal{M}_{6,n}^{3}\cup PQ_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP and <math id="S10.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}=R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}=R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof is now nearly identical to Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmthm13" title="Lemma 10.13. ‣ 10.5. Conclusion when 
          
            
              =
              g
              5 ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.13</span></a>.
The only difference is to note that, by excision, Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 8.4. ‣ 8.2. Our Hurwitz spaces ‣ 8. Faber–Pandharipande Hurwitz (FPH) cycles ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a> remains valid upon restricting to the complement of <math id="S10.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}(PQ_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}(PQ_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> in the domain and <math id="S10.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the target.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.7. </span>Final step: filling in</h3>

<div id="S10.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lemmas <a href="#S9.Thmthm11" title="Lemma 9.11. ‣ 9.3. Generators for the Chow ring ‣ 9. Trigonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.11</span></a>, <a href="#S10.Thmthm13" title="Lemma 10.13. ‣ 10.5. Conclusion when 
          
            
              =
              g
              5 ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.13</span></a> and <a href="#S10.Thmthm14" title="Lemma 10.14. ‣ 10.6. Conclusion when 
          
            
              =
              g
              6 ‣ 10. Tetragonal curves ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.14</span></a> add three more columns of open circles (pictured in blue) to our chart, giving the chart on the left below. We also have an open circle for <math id="S10.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> (pictured in black) by our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>. (It is clear that each of the strata involved in the proof given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> has the CKgP, being a quotient of an open subset of affine space by a suitable group.)
Using the filling criteria (Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 4.1 (Filling criterion, version 1). ‣ 4. Filling criteria ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> for <math id="S10.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3 (Thickening criterion, for compact type). ‣ 4. Filling criteria ‣ On the Chow and cohomology rings of moduli spaces of stable curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> for <math id="S10.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ct</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}^{\ct}</annotation></semantics></math>) we obtain the chart on the right.</p>
